To:&nbsp;Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;
Subject:&nbsp;[DRAFT] Re: Report
&nbsp;


Dear Michael,&nbsp;Priyanka, Sarah,&nbsp;

I&nbsp;refer to your email&nbsp;of&nbsp;17 October 2025&nbsp;containing&nbsp;what you term a protected disclosure.&nbsp;

As per my email on Tuesday 21&nbsp;October 2025, thank you once again for raising these points.&nbsp;We take seriously any professional and regulatory concerns raised by anyone at the Firm&nbsp;and&nbsp;&nbsp;encourage&nbsp;employees to raise matters with which they are not happy, provided
 they are doing so in good faith. To that end&nbsp;I&nbsp;have thoroughly investigated your concerns&nbsp;in my role as&nbsp;COLP&nbsp;and&nbsp;my&nbsp;findings are set out&nbsp;below.&nbsp;Please be aware that there is no obligation&nbsp;under the&nbsp;Whistleblowing &nbsp;policy&nbsp;to report back to you on&nbsp;the my&nbsp;findings
 however, in all the circumstances, I thought it would be helpful for you if I did so.&nbsp;

Context&nbsp;

Stewarts&nbsp;was&nbsp;originally instructed by PG to&nbsp;assist&nbsp;our costs team on the BHP litigation.&nbsp;The scope of&nbsp;its&nbsp;work then expanded to include the NOx litigations.&nbsp;Stewarts provided excellent services to the firm&nbsp;– as&nbsp;I am&nbsp;sure Sarah can attest –&nbsp;and&nbsp;accrued&nbsp;substantial&nbsp;fees
 in doing so.&nbsp;However, despite that, Mr Goodhead (then in control of the firm) disputed&nbsp;Stewarts’&nbsp;fees and refused to settle them.&nbsp;Stewarts, quite understandably, required settlement of&nbsp;its&nbsp;fees to keep working.&nbsp;Given the value of&nbsp;its&nbsp;work, it was agreed with
 Gramercy that&nbsp;it&nbsp;would pay Stewarts’ arrears and, thereafter, instruct Stewarts directly.&nbsp;

Stewarts&nbsp;then&nbsp;acted for Gramercy but continued to provide input on certain limited costs&nbsp;matters.&nbsp;This included obtaining their input onto the drafting of the Litigation Management Agreement (the&nbsp;“LMA”) and Agency Agreement (the&nbsp;“AA”) referenced
 in your email.&nbsp;

The position of litigation funders&nbsp;

I&nbsp;understand that the central premise of your&nbsp;allegations&nbsp;is that litigation funders may only have an interest in, have sight of, and offer their views on, matters that – in your words – “(a) give rise to a financial liability on the funder, or (b) relate
 to compliance with the funding terms”.&nbsp;It is noted however that your letter does not provide any authority to support this.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

In&nbsp;my&nbsp;view,&nbsp;your central premise&nbsp;does&nbsp;not&nbsp;reflect&nbsp;the correct position.&nbsp;As Tomlinson LJ explained in
Excalibur Ventures LLC v Texas Keystone Inc [2016] EWCA&nbsp;Civ&nbsp;1144&nbsp;at [31]:&nbsp;

“Litigation funding is an accepted and judicially sanctioned activity perceived to be in the public interest. What the judge characterised as ‘rigorous analysis of law, facts and witnesses, consideration of proportionality and review at&nbsp;appropriate intervals’&nbsp;is
 what is to be expected of a responsible funder … and cannot of itself be&nbsp;champertous. I agree … that, rather than interfering with the due administration of justice, if anything such activities promote the due administration of justice.&nbsp;For the avoidance of&nbsp;doubt&nbsp;I
 should mention that ongoing review of the progress of litigation through the medium of lawyers independent of those conducting litigation… seems to me not just prudent but often essential&nbsp;in order to&nbsp;reduce the risk of orders for indemnity being made against
 the unsuccessfully funded party.”&nbsp;

It is thus clear that responsible funders are&nbsp;encouraged by the Courts&nbsp;to take an active interest in,&nbsp;and actively review,&nbsp;the proceedings which they fund, including via their own solicitors, such as, in this case,&nbsp;Stewarts.&nbsp;

Furthermore, there are no&nbsp;hard and fast&nbsp;rules&nbsp;regarding&nbsp;the scope of&nbsp;a funder’s&nbsp;interest in and review of the&nbsp;proceedings.&nbsp;Rather, the question is whether the terms of the funding agreement&nbsp;“would undermine the purity of justice or would corrupt public justice”
 with “the crucial issue” being “whether the non-party can exercise excessive control or influence over the conduct of the proceedings in such a way as, for example, to supress evidence, influence witnesses, or procure an improper settlement”
 (see Gwynneth Knowles J at [36] in Akhmedov v Akhmedov [2020] EWHC 1526 (Fam)&nbsp;paraphrasing the Court of Appeal in
Sibthorpe&nbsp;v Southwark LBC [2011] EWCA&nbsp;Civ&nbsp;25&nbsp;and&nbsp;quoting&nbsp;Snowden J (as he was then) in
Davey v Money [2019] EWHC 997 (Ch)).&nbsp;

I&nbsp;do not consider that any&nbsp;of that applies here. Indeed, the&nbsp;recoverability&nbsp;of costs between solicitors, which is the subject of the LMA and AA&nbsp;that&nbsp;Stewarts were&nbsp;commenting on, is quintessentially an issue that a funder would legitimately&nbsp;take an interest
 in.&nbsp;

I&nbsp;would also remind you that the consequence of any conduct that crosses the line is that the relevant litigation funding agreement becomes&nbsp;unenforceable&nbsp;(see
Farrar v&nbsp;Candey [2022] EWCA&nbsp;Civ&nbsp;295&nbsp;per Arnold LJ at [30]).&nbsp;It&nbsp;does not constitute&nbsp;a defence to the proceedings, nor provide grounds for strike out or a stay&nbsp;(see
Martell v Consett Iron Co Ltd&nbsp;[1955] Ch 363&nbsp;per Jenkins LJ at 421-422 and
Abraham v Thompson [1997] CLC 1370&nbsp;per Potter LJ at 1385-1386).&nbsp;Rather, it means that the funder and the funded solicitors cannot recover their costs against their clients nor,&nbsp;under the indemnity principle, against the defendants. It is
 thus the funder and funded solicitors that would suffer prejudice, not the clients.&nbsp;

Your&nbsp;concerns&nbsp;

In light of&nbsp;the foregoing,&nbsp;I&nbsp;do not accept the central premise of your email that there has been any impropriety around the negotiation of the LMA and AA.&nbsp;

Stewarts,&nbsp;on behalf of Gramercy,&nbsp;reviewed the LMA and AA.&nbsp;It&nbsp;then&nbsp;provided&nbsp;its expert&nbsp;thoughts and input as to what Gramercy thought of those documents&nbsp;for PG’s consideration in&nbsp;the&nbsp;note dated 28 May 2025.&nbsp;That note clearly&nbsp;states&nbsp;that it “sets out our initial
 observations” and that “[f]or the avoidance of doubt our advice is to Gramercy rather than PG who we understand are being separately advised on the drafting of the CCAA by counsel, George MacDonald.&nbsp;We are content for Gramercy to&nbsp;share, or&nbsp;instruct
 us to share this advice note with PG, but in doing so we assume no responsibility or duties to PG”.&nbsp;It is also clear from that note that Stewarts was lacking much of the relevant background,&nbsp;context&nbsp;and documents.&nbsp;

With regards to&nbsp;Mr Chamberlayne’s email of 9 September 2025 at 11.30am, Sarah’s extract&nbsp;does not&nbsp;contain&nbsp;a direction or instruction requiring PG to adopt a particular course of action.&nbsp;Instead, it simply recorded that Gramercy was “content” with the
 language of the revised AA.&nbsp;PG&nbsp;(which&nbsp;was receiving separate advice)&nbsp;was free to disagree with&nbsp;Gramercy’s&nbsp;recorded&nbsp;position, but&nbsp;did not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

In this respect, it is unclear whether Sarah is saying that it was wrong for her – acting on behalf of PG as the lead for costs – to agree with Stewarts’ suggestion.&nbsp;That suggestion is,&nbsp;I&nbsp;understand, set out in&nbsp;paragraph&nbsp;2.10 of&nbsp;its&nbsp;note of 28 May 2025, namely:&nbsp;

“Deemed agreement – Clause 60 states that unless the Principal Solicitor, gives the Lead Solicitors written notice by 4pm on 30 May 2025 that the Lead Solicitors are no longer authorised to act for your&nbsp;Clients&nbsp;then, irrespective of whether this Agreement
 is signed it will be&nbsp;deemed&nbsp;to be agreed.&nbsp;That is potentially contentious and provides&nbsp;a very short&nbsp;notice for&nbsp;deemed&nbsp;agreement to a lengthy,&nbsp;complex&nbsp;and important agreement.&nbsp;It presents&nbsp;a high risk&nbsp;of challenge.”&nbsp;

You have not&nbsp;identified&nbsp;any reason to suggest this is not&nbsp;a helpful and sensible concern&nbsp;identified&nbsp;by Stewarts.&nbsp;I&nbsp;also&nbsp;consider&nbsp;that&nbsp;it&nbsp;is clearly not something&nbsp;that&nbsp;could&nbsp;be said to be “excessive control or influence” that might&nbsp;“corrupt
 public justice”.&nbsp;In fact,&nbsp;I&nbsp;consider&nbsp;it to&nbsp;be&nbsp;the opposite, viz., a&nbsp;wholly reasonable&nbsp;point for consideration.&nbsp;

It is thus unclear to&nbsp;me&nbsp;whether or&nbsp;why Sarah says that&nbsp;Stewarts’ suggestion&nbsp;should not have been accepted by PG or LD as a prudent amendment.&nbsp;If there was some reasoned objection,&nbsp;please can she explain&nbsp;(i) what that objection was, (ii) why she&nbsp;did not disagree&nbsp;with
 the suggestion and&nbsp;proceed&nbsp;in the manner she thought best, and (iii)&nbsp;why she did not raise an objection at the time?&nbsp;Her&nbsp;references to “numerous calls, redrafts, and escalations”&nbsp;do not&nbsp;allow&nbsp;me&nbsp;to assess&nbsp;the matter&nbsp;properly.&nbsp;

In any event, under the terms of the firm’s funding agreements with Gramercy, it may not direct PG as to&nbsp;how to conduct the litigation.&nbsp;It may actively review the conduct of the proceedings and&nbsp;provide&nbsp;its&nbsp;views (as is encouraged by the Courts), but the ultimate
 decisions&nbsp;regarding&nbsp;matters of&nbsp;conduct&nbsp;of the litigation&nbsp;are&nbsp;for PG.&nbsp;&nbsp;

I&nbsp;do not, therefore, accept your suggestion that “PG is now openly operating in a world&nbsp;where&nbsp;the funder of the firm has direct management/control of aspects of the litigation”.&nbsp;Not only it is not&nbsp;true&nbsp;but the&nbsp;allegation is&nbsp;simply&nbsp;not supported by&nbsp;evidence.&nbsp;You
 are each senior lawyers within our firm and it is incumbent on you each to not make allegations that are not properly justifiable on the evidence or the law.

In sum, and as you can see from the detailed response above,&nbsp;I&nbsp;have carefully considered the stated concerns on their face and have concluded that they do not&nbsp;indicate&nbsp;any breach or wrongdoing.&nbsp;I&nbsp;trust that this answers your concerns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Your queries&nbsp;

Please now see below my&nbsp;response&nbsp;to your three queries as follows:&nbsp;



why representations have been made to the Court,&nbsp;Defendants&nbsp;and staff that Gramercy do not have any role in the conduct,&nbsp;control&nbsp;or management of the NOx Group Litigations when plainly - they do and continue to do so; &nbsp;
Because that representation was correct. Gramercy does not have any role in the conduct,&nbsp;control&nbsp;or management of the proceedings; sole authority for the conduct,&nbsp;control&nbsp;or management rests with PG.&nbsp;Gramercy’s&nbsp;involvement is limited to actively reviewing
 the proceedings from time to time as is proper and encouraged by the Courts.&nbsp;


whether the Board intends to update the Court as to the true position; and&nbsp;&nbsp;
No because there is no need.&nbsp;


whether the Board intends to inform the clients and other claimant firms of&nbsp;Gramercy's&nbsp;input into documents that have a direct bearing on costs and client instructions.&nbsp;&nbsp;
No, again, because there is no need.&nbsp;





I&nbsp;trust that this settles the matter.&nbsp;Should you wish to discuss further,&nbsp;I&nbsp;would be happy to arrange a meeting.&nbsp;




Kind regards,&nbsp;

Alicia&nbsp;












